# Layered architecture

Use the real Maven modules in the repository as the source of truth.

The application is organized in layers:

```
Presentation (@Controller + JSP/tag files) -> webapp
Services (@Service)                        -> services
Persistence (@Repository + JDBC)           -> persistence
Domain                                     -> models
```

## Real Maven modules

```
paw-webapp/               <- parent pom
|- webapp/                <- controllers, WebConfig, JSPs, tag files, CSS, JS, web forms, i18n
|- services/              <- service implementations
|- service-contracts/     <- service interfaces
|- persistence/           <- JDBC implementations, tests, migrations
|- persistence-contracts/ <- DAO interfaces
`- models/                <- domain entities
```

There is no generic `interfaces/` module. In this repository, interfaces live in
`*-contracts` modules.

## Module dependencies

- `webapp` depends on `service-contracts` and `services`.
- `services` depends on `service-contracts` and `persistence-contracts`; `persistence`
  is included as `runtime`.
- `persistence` depends on `persistence-contracts`.
- `service-contracts` and `persistence-contracts` depend on `models`.
- Never import concrete implementations from another layer when a contract exists.

## Layer rules

- `webapp` injects service interfaces, for example `ClassUserService`.
- `services` injects DAO interfaces, for example `ClassUserDao`.
- `persistence` contains SQL, `JdbcTemplate`, `SimpleJdbcInsert`, and `RowMapper`.
- `models` must not depend on Spring MVC or JDBC.
- JSPs and tag files only consume model data; they must not access services.
- Web-layer form objects, validation, i18n, shared CSS, and minimal interactive JS
  belong in `webapp`.

## WebConfig

`webapp/.../config/WebConfig.java` centralizes web and runtime configuration:

- It performs `@ComponentScan` for controllers, services, and persistence.
- It configures `ViewResolver` with `prefix="/WEB-INF/views/"` and `suffix=".jsp"`.
- It configures the production `DataSource` with PostgreSQL.
- It runs Flyway from `classpath:db/migration`.
- It exposes static resources from `/css/**`.
- It defines supporting web beans such as `MessageSource` and other MVC-related
  configuration for forms and i18n.

Because of this, controllers should return logical view names (`"index"`,
`"marketplace"`, `"helloworld/index"`) instead of repeating the full JSP path,
unless there is a specific exception in existing code.

## Database and testing

- Production/runtime: PostgreSQL.
- Service tests: JUnit 5 + Mockito.
- Persistence tests: Spring Test + in-memory HSQLDB.

Do not assume HSQLDB is the main application database. It is only used for tests.

## Web-layer conventions

This architecture includes:

- `*Form` classes in the web layer;
- JSR-303 validation for form submission;
- `messages.properties`-based i18n;
- reusable frontend components;
- a `main.css` design system file with semantic color and UI tokens;
- minimal JavaScript for modern interactive behavior and `localStorage` UX state.

All of these belong to the existing server-rendered Spring MVC architecture.

## Violations to avoid

- Injecting a DAO directly into a controller.
- Writing SQL outside `persistence`.
- Inventing modules or packages that do not exist in this repository.
- Documenting `WEB-INF/jsp` or `jsp/components` when the repository uses
  `WEB-INF/views` and `WEB-INF/tags`.
